WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.311
Mark Heidelberger, Producer: I just need three
things from you: I need a script, I need a

00:00:05.311 --> 00:00:09.601
budget and I need you to get the hell out
of my way.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:10.601
ONE WEEK FROM TONIGHT

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:11.601
I’m not a huge fan of academics.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.530
I’m not a huge fan of people who just teach
and haven’t done in 20 years.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.720
I like to learn from the people who are doing
it now.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.070
ON TUESDAY, NOVEMBER 13TH, 2018

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.869
You can’t just give me a script “How much
does it cost?”

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:24.699
“Well is Matt Damon in your movie?

00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:25.699
Great!

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:26.699
Your budget just went up by 10 million dollars.”

00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:30.439
I can make an argument to do this movie by
one million bucks or 10 million bucks.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:32.739
So really it’s like “So how much can you
raise?

00:00:32.739 --> 00:00:36.120
Or how much do you have?”

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.970
And then if they really don’t have a good
idea of who they see in it, how wide do you

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:43.910
see it being distributed but I have to get
an idea of what range and how big they are

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:44.910
seeing this in their head.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:49.829
WE INVITE YOU TO JOIN US AND PRODUCER MARK
HEIDELBERGER

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.370
If you just want to go out and make a piece
of art for yourself and you have the money

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.320
to blow, go ahead.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.460
But that doesn’t mean you are going to be
part of the entertainment industry.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.649
It doesn’t mean you are going to be part
of the film…business.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:02.559
It is a business as well as a commodity as
well as art.

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:04.940
That’s where people get hung up - it’s
art, right.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.020
But it’s also a commodity.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.510
FOR A ONE NIGHT ONLY EVENT

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:15.010
I read an article the other day (maybe it
was like a month ago) it was like a cartoon.

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.010
My head was like VROOOOM!

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.430
I was so annoyed with this article.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:21.450
It just started with “Okay, now you’ve
finished your film.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.590
Now it’s time to start thinking about distribution.”

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:24.590
I’m like “No!

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:25.590
No!

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:26.590
No!

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.750
It’s not time to start, you should have
been thinking about distribution ages ago.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:32.180
From the script stage you should have been
thinking about distribution.”

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:37.260
And I’ve told people, the smart way to do
it (and they always have a script that they

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.550
just really want to do) and I’m like “Well
have you considered your audience?

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.220
Have you considered whether there is an audience
for it?

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.770
Have you considered how you are going to get
that audience?

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:48.010
Have you considered how big that audience
is and whether it justifies the budget you

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.990
want to spend?

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.270
Have you looked at the market, what other
films are out there in the market?

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.410
How oversaturated that is.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.050
I can’t tell you how many times I hear people
say “I’m going to do horror because it’s

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.440
so cheap,” so you can make a bunch of money
off of it.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.190
You’re only considering one thing, you’re
only considering budget.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:05.040
EVENT INFO BELOW

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.550
When I read the script I’m thinking about
story.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.890
I’m thinking about the arc of the characters,
I’m thinking about conflict and I’m thinking

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:13.860
about why are we telling this story but I’m
also thinking about “Okay, who would we

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.060
get to play these roles on this budget level?

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.860
What are some decent names?

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.750
Who am I picturing in this?

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:26.550
I’m presuming in this situation that the
movie is real, that it’s not just a script.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.390
That I’m not just reading it for fun like
we’re making this picture.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.140
This picture is funded, Mark.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:33.140
Read the script.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.390
So I’m reading it and I’m like “Okay,
how are we going to pull off this…we need

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:39.050
this grandiose house?”

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:44.020
STRAIGHT TALK ABOUT FEATURE FILM DEVELOPMENT
AND PRODUCTION - A NIGHT WITH MARK HEIDELBERGER

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:49.819
It’s a lot easier to develop a story in
synopsis form or treatment form then it is

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:52.800
to keep rewriting the script when there is
fundamental problems with it.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.220
You don’t start building the building “Ahhh?

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.190
It’s slanted…alright let’s start taking
out some bricks and try to get it straight.”

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.620
You work all of that in the design phase,
the blueprint phase.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:00.620
So that’s what I try to get them to.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:06.700
And the 12 questions to me is Okay, that’s
that beginning, that organic seed that we

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.880
need to plant to make a healthy tree grow.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.160
What’s your theme?

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.740
What’s your thematic question?

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:13.480
Why are you telling this?

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.569
The thematic question is what you pose to
the audience.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:18.630
It could be a moral question or something
else.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:22.950
But some question you want them to think about
like “Is family really just people you’re

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:23.950
related to?

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:24.950
Or is it something more?”

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.130
It can be your thematic question.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:31.180
It’s not overtly stated in the question
but it’s in the film’s subconscious, it’s

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.560
what your film is kind of really about.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.140
And then your theme is how you answer that
question by the end.

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:37.950
That’s how you answer your thematic question.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.200
So what are those things?

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.690
I have a great story I think I can tell within
that and consulting with distributors and

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:49.780
sales agents who know the market place and
if you’re afraid to get the sales agents

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:56.260
and distributors involved early and find out
what the market looks like and what buyers

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:57.500
are looking for.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:03.610
And what budget ranges but if you don’t
want to do that and you make a picture that

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:08.989
makes no money, really you don’t have anyone
to blame but yourself.

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:13.970
The end use, the marketing, distribution,
publicity, festivals (all of that), you should

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:17.900
be looking at that from the beginning, from
inception of your story.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.879
Just like you trust a DP to shoot your picture
because he knows more about lighting and camera

00:04:21.879 --> 00:04:22.879
than you do.

00:04:22.879 --> 00:04:26.220
Or an editor because he knows a lot more about
cutting than you do.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:29.529
Distributors and sales agents know more about
selling in the market than you do.

00:04:29.529 --> 00:04:34.400
And so if you want a better chance at getting
some sort of return, involve people that know

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:36.800
more about what they do than you do about
what they do.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.979
I say this all the time when I hire people.

00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:41.900
I say “Look, you know more about what you
do than I know about what you do.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.240
So I’m going to tell you the results I want,
when I want it by, I’m going to tell you

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.740
and I’m going to tell you what resources
I’m going to give you to get me that result.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:49.889
If you say “I can do it.”

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.210
I’ll say “Great, go do it.”

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:57.139
And I’m here to support you or “Hey, I
need you to do this or that as long as it’s

00:04:57.139 --> 00:04:58.139
in these parameters, done!

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:03.610
If there’s a problem, we’ll fix it and
we’ll make sure it doesn’t happen again.”

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:04.610
Trust but verify.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.689
But I don’t stand over their shoulders.

00:05:07.689 --> 00:05:11.659
I hired you because this is what you do and
you’re better at it than I am, so go do

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:12.659
it.

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:14.280
And the crew loves that!

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.719
Straight Talk About Feature Film Development
And Production.

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:27.820
A Night With Mark Heidelberger on November
13th, 2018 in North Hollywood, CA.

